  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><newline/><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<para>1. Lin is solving this system of equations: </para>
<para>\( \begin {cases} 6x-5y=34\\3x+2y=8 \end {cases} \)</para>
<para>She starts by rearranging the second equation to isolate the \(y\) variable: \( y = 4 - 1.5x \). She then substitutes the expression \(4 - 1.5x\) for \( y \) in the first equation, as shown:</para>
<para>\( 6x-5(4-1.5x)=34 \)\( 6x-20-7.5x=34 \)</para>
<para>\( -1.5x=54 \)
  \( x=-36 \)</para>

<para>\( y=4-1.5x \)</para>
<para>\( y=4-1.5 \cdot(-36) \)</para>
<para>\( y=58 \)</para>
<para>Does Lin’s solution of \((-36, 58)\) makes both equations in the system true?</para>
<list>
  <item>yes; Her solution makes both equations true.</item>
  <item>No; Her solution does not make both equations true.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> No; Her solution does not make both equations true. </para>
<para>2. Which of the following is<emphasis> true </emphasis>of Lin’s solution?</para>
<list>
  <item>Lin made a multiplication error. The correct answer is <emphasis>x</emphasis> = 4 and <emphasis>y </emphasis>= -2, or (4,-2)</item>
  <item>Lin made a multiplication error. The correct answer is <emphasis>x</emphasis> = 9 and <emphasis>y </emphasis>= 4, or (9,4)</item>
  <item>Lin made an addition error. The correct answer is <emphasis>x</emphasis> = 0 and <emphasis>y </emphasis>= 4, or (0,4)</item>
  <item>Lin’s solution of (-36, 58) is correct.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Lin made a multiplication error. The correct answer is <emphasis>x</emphasis> = 4 and <emphasis>y </emphasis>= -2, or (4,-2)</para>
<para>In 3 - 9, solve the systems of equations by substitution.</para>
<span>
  <para>3. \(\left\{\begin{array}{l}2x\;+\;y\;=\;-2\\3x\;-\;y\;=\;7\end{array}\right.\)</para>

<list>
  <item>(−1,0) </item>
  <item>(0,−2) </item>
  <item>(1,−4) </item>
  <item>(2,6)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\( (1,-4) \)</para>
	<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}2x+y&amp;=&amp;-2
		 \\ 2(1)+(-4)&amp;=&amp;-2
		 \\ 2-4&amp;=&amp;-2
		 \\ -2&amp;=&amp;-2\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		
		<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}3x-y&amp;=&amp;7
		 \\ 3(1)-(-4)&amp;=&amp;7
		 \\ 3+4&amp;=&amp;7
		 \\ 7&amp;=&amp;7\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		<para>(1,-4) Makes both equations in the system true, therefore it is a solution.</para>
<para>4. \(\left\{\begin{array}{l}x\;-\;3y\;=\;-9\\2x\;+\;5y\;=\;4\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<list>
  <item>(−6,1) </item>
  <item>(2,0)</item>
  <item>(0,3)</item>
  <item>(−3,2)</item>
</list><span>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( (-3,2) \)</para>
	<para>Worked Solution.</para>
		<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}x−3y&amp;=&amp;−9
		 \\ (-3)−3(2)&amp;=&amp;−9
		 \\ -3−6=&amp;−&amp;9
		 \\ -9&amp;=&amp;-9\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		
		<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}2x+5y=4
		 \\ 2(-3)+5(2)&amp;=&amp;4
		 \\ -6+10&amp;=&amp;4
		 \\ 4&amp;=&amp;4\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		<para>(-3,2) Makes both equations in the system true, therefore it is a solution.</para>
  <para>5.  \(\left\{\begin{array}{l}-2x\;+\;2y\;=\;6\\2y\;=\;-6x\;+\;14\end{array}\right.\)</para>

<list>
  <item>(1,4)</item>
  <item>(0,7)</item>
  <item>(2,1)</item>
  <item>(−3,0)</item>
</list>
<para><b>Answer:</b> (1,4)</para><span>
	<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}−2x+2y&amp;=&amp;6
		 \\ −2(1)+2(4)&amp;=&amp;6
		 \\ −2+8&amp;=&amp;6
		 \\ 6&amp;=&amp;6\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		
		<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}2y&amp;=&amp;−6x+14
		 \\ 2(4)&amp;=&amp;−6(1)+14
		 \\ 8&amp;=&amp;−6+14
		 \\ 8&amp;=&amp;8\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		<para>(1,4) Makes both equations in the system true, therefore it is a solution.</para>
  <para>6. \(\left\{\begin{array}{l}3x\;+\;4y\;=\;1\\y\;=\;-\frac25x\;+\;2\end{array}\right.\)</para>
  <list>
    <item>(5,0)</item>
    <item>(0,2)</item>
    <item>(−1,1)</item>
    <item>(−5,4)</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( (-5,4) \)</para>
	  <para>Worked Solution.</para>
		<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl} 3x+4y&amp;=&amp;1
		 \\ 3(-5)+4(4)&amp;=&amp;1
		 \\ −15+16&amp;=&amp;1
		 \\ 6&amp;=&amp;6\checkmark
			\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		
		 <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl} y&amp;=&amp;−25x+2
		 \\ 4&amp;=&amp;−25(-5)+2
		 \\ 4&amp;=&amp;2+2
		 \\ 4&amp;=&amp;4\checkmark
			 \\  \end{array}\)</para>
		<para>(−5,4) Makes both equations in the system true, therefore it is a solution.</para>
  <para>7. \(\left\{\begin{array}{l}4x\;+\;y\;=\;10\\\;x\;-\;2y\;=\;-20\end{array}\right.\)</para>
  <list>
    <item>(2,2)</item>
    <item>(0,10)</item>
    <item>(−4,8)</item>
    <item>(4,12)</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( (0,10) \)</para>
	  <para>Worked Solution.</para>
		<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}4x+y&amp;=&amp;10
		 \\ 4(0)+(10)&amp;=&amp;10
		 \\ 0+10&amp;=&amp;10
		 \\ 10&amp;=&amp;10\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		
		<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}x−2y&amp;=&amp;−20
		 \\ 0−2(10)&amp;=&amp;−20
		 \\ 0−20&amp;=&amp;−20
		 \\ -20&amp;=&amp;-20\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		<para>(0,10)  Makes both equations in the system true, therefore it is a solution.</para>
  <para>8. \(\left\{\begin{array}{l}y\;=\;x\;-\;6\\\;y\;=-\frac32x\;+\;4\end{array}\right.\)</para>
  <list>
    <item>(6,0)</item>
    <item>(2,1)</item>
    <item>(4,−2)</item>
    <item>(0,4)</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( (4,-2) \)</para>
	<para>Worked Solution.</para>
		<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}y&amp;=&amp;x−6
		 \\ -2&amp;=&amp;(4)−6
		 \\ -2&amp;=&amp;−2\checkmark
			\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		
		<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}y&amp;=&amp;−32x+4
		 \\ -2&amp;=&amp;−32(4)+4
		 \\ -2&amp;=&amp;−6+4
		 \\ -2&amp;=&amp;-2\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		<para>(4,−2) Makes both equations in the system true, therefore it is a solution.</para>
  <para>9.  \(\left\{\begin{array}{l}2x\;-\;16y\;=\;8\\-x\;-\;8y\;=\;-4\;\end{array}\right.\)</para>
  <list>
    <item>(12,1)</item>
    <item>(4,0)</item>
    <item>(−4,1)</item>
    <item>(−4,−1)</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( (4,0) \)</para>
	  <para>Worked Solution.</para>
	<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}	2x−16y&amp;=&amp;8
		 \\ 2(4)−16(0)&amp;=&amp;8
		 \\ 8-0&amp;=&amp;8
		 \\ 8&amp;=&amp;8\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		
	<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}	−x−8y&amp;=&amp;−4
		 \\ −4−8(0)&amp;=&amp;−4
		 \\ -4−0&amp;=&amp;−4
		 \\ -4&amp;=&amp;-4\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
	<para>	(4,0) Makes both equations in the system true, therefore it is a solution.</para>

<para>10.  \(\left\{\begin{array}{l}y\;=\;-\frac23x\;+\;6\\2x\;-\;y\;=\;2\;\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<list>
  <item>(0,6)</item>
  <item>(−2,0)</item>
  <item>(3,4)</item>
  <item>(6,2)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( (3, 4) \)</para>
	<para>	Worked Solution.</para>
		<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}y&amp;=&amp;−\frac23x+6
		 \\ 4&amp;=&amp;−\frac23(3)+6
		 \\ 4&amp;=&amp;−2+6
		 \\ 4&amp;=&amp;4\checkmark\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		
		<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}	2(3)−(4)&amp;=&amp;2
		 \\ 6−4&amp;=&amp;2
		 \\ 2&amp;=&amp;2\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		<para>(3,4)  Makes both equations in the system true, therefore it is a solution.</para>
<para>11. Tyler and Han are trying to solve this system by substitution:</para><span id="docs-internal-guid-69eeabcd-7fff-73e4-c2b7-5a427adf1ddf"><span data-mathml="&lt;math xmlns=&quot;http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML&quot; display=&quot;inline&quot;&gt;&lt;semantics&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mo&gt;{&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mtable&gt;&lt;mtr&gt;&lt;mtd columnalign=&quot;left&quot;&gt;&lt;mi&gt;x&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo&gt;&amp;#x2212;&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mn&gt;4&lt;/mn&gt;&lt;mi&gt;y&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo&gt;=&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mn&gt;&amp;#x2212;4&lt;/mn&gt;&lt;/mtd&gt;&lt;/mtr&gt;&lt;mtr&gt;&lt;mtd columnalign=&quot;left&quot;&gt;&lt;mo&gt;&amp;#x2212;&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mn&gt;3&lt;/mn&gt;&lt;mi&gt;x&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo&gt;+&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mn&gt;4&lt;/mn&gt;&lt;mi&gt;y&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo&gt;=&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mn&gt;0&lt;/mn&gt;&lt;/mtd&gt;&lt;/mtr&gt;&lt;/mtable&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;/semantics&gt;&lt;/math&gt;" id="MathJax-Element-9-Frame" role="presentation" tabindex="0">
    <nobr aria-hidden="true"><span id="MathJax-Span-109"><span id="MathJax-Span-110"><span id="MathJax-Span-111"><span id="MathJax-Span-112"><span id="MathJax-Span-113"/></span></span></span></span></nobr>
  
<para id="fs-id1167826978106"><math display="inline">
    <semantics>
      <mrow>
        <mrow>
          <mrow>
            <mo/>
          </mrow>
        </mrow>
      </mrow>
    </semantics>
  </math></para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}x+3y=-5\\9x+3y=3\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<mtable>
  <mtr>
    <mtd columnalign="left"/>
  </mtr>
</mtable>
<mo/>
<annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content">
  <mrow>
    <mrow>
      <mo>{</mo>
      <mtable>
        <mtr>
          <mtd columnalign="left">
            <mi>y</mi>
            <mo>=</mo>
            <mo>−</mo>
            <mn>3</mn>
            <mi>x</mi>
            <mo>−</mo>
            <mn>6</mn>
          </mtd>
        </mtr>
        <mtr>
          <mtd columnalign="left">
            <mn>6</mn>
            <mi>x</mi>
            <mo>+</mo>
            <mn>2</mn>
            <mi>y</mi>
            <mo>=</mo>
            <mo>−</mo>
            <mn>12</mn>
          </mtd>
        </mtr>
      </mtable>
    </mrow>
    <mo>.</mo>
  </mrow>
</annotation-xml>
<para>Tyler’s first step is to isolate \(x\) in the first equation to get \( x = -5 - 3y \).</para>
<para>Han’s first step is to isolate \(3y\) in the first equation to get \( 3y = -5 - x \).</para>
<para>Solve using both methods to show that both first
    steps can be used to solve the system and will yield the same solution.</para>
<para>What is the solution to the system?</para>
<list>
  <item>(1,-2)</item>
  <item>(0,1)</item>
  <item>(4,-3)</item>
  <item>(-5,0)</item>
</list>
<para><b>Answer: </b>(1,-2)</para>
		<para>Worked Solution.</para>
	<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}	x+3y&amp;=&amp;−5
		 \\ 1+3(-2)&amp;=&amp;−5
		 \\ 1-6&amp;=&amp;−5
		 \\ -5&amp;=&amp;-5\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		
	<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}	9x+3y&amp;=&amp;3
		 \\ 9(1)+3(-2)&amp;=&amp;3
		 \\ 9-6&amp;=&amp;3
		 \\ 3&amp;=&amp;3\checkmark
		\\  \end{array}\)</para>
		<para>(1,-2) Makes both equations in the system true, therefore it is a solution.</para>


</span></span></span></span></span></section>
